"To determine the number of vertical asymptotes of the graph of \\( y = \\frac{2}{x^2+x-6} \\), we need to find the values of \\( x \\) that make the denominator zero, as vertical asymptotes occur where the function is undefined.\n\nThe denominator of the function is \\( x^2 + x - 6 \\). We set this equal to zero and solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x^2 + x - 6 = 0 \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation, and we can factor it:\n\n\\[ (x + 3)(x - 2) = 0 \\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[ x + 3 = 0 \\implies x = -3 \\]\n\\[ x - 2 = 0 \\implies x = 2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the values of \\( x \\) that make the denominator zero are \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\). These are the vertical asymptotes of the function.\n\nThus, the number of vertical asymptotes is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."
