"According to Daniel's theory, the popularity of a television is inversely proportional to its cost. This means that if the cost of a television increases, the number of sales decreases, and vice versa.\n\nLet's denote the number of customers who would buy a television that costs $2500 as \\( x \\).\n\nGiven:\n- 15 customers buy a television that costs $1500.\n\nSince the popularity is inversely proportional to the cost, we can set up the following relationship:\n\\[ \\text{Number of sales} \\propto \\frac{1}{\\text{Cost}} \\]\n\nThis means:\n\\[ 15 \\propto \\frac{1}{1500} \\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we need to use the same relationship for the television that costs $2500:\n\\[ x \\propto \\frac{1}{2500} \\]\n\nSince the proportionality constant is the same for both cases, we can equate the two expressions:\n\\[ x = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of customers who would buy a television that costs $2500 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\)."
