"To determine how much greater \\(43^2\\) is compared to \\(27^2\\), we first need to calculate the values of \\(27^2\\) and \\(43^2\\).\n\n1. Calculate \\(27^2\\):\n\\[\n27^2 = 27 \\times 27 = 729\n\\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(43^2\\):\n\\[\n43^2 = 43 \\times 43 = 1849\n\\]\n\n3. Find the difference between \\(43^2\\) and \\(27^2\\):\n\\[\n43^2 - 27^2 = 1849 - 729 = 1120\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(43^2\\) is \\(\\boxed{1120}\\) greater than \\(27^2\\)."
"We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = 1\n\\]\n\nTo find the value of \\((p-1)(q-1)\\), we can use the identity:\n\\[\n(p-1)(q-1) = pq - p - q + 1\n\\]\n\nWe know from the given equation that:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = \\frac{p+q}{pq} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\np+q = pq\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(p+q = pq\\) into the identity for \\((p-1)(q-1)\\):\n\\[\n(p-1)(q-1) = pq - p - q + 1 = pq - pq + 1\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\\[\n(p-1)(q-1) = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\((p-1)(q-1)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]"
