"First, we need to find the slope of the line containing the points $(-1,2)$ and $(1, -2)$. The slope of a line passing through two points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} \\]\n\nSubstituting the given points:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{-2 - 2}{1 - (-1)} = \\frac{-4}{2} = -2 \\]\n\nThe slope of a line perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the original line. Therefore, the slope of the perpendicular line is:\n\n\\[ \\text{slope of perpendicular line} = -\\frac{1}{m} = \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\nThus, the slope of the line perpendicular to the line containing the points $(-1,2)$ and $(1, -2)$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}$."
"To find the point of intersection of the lines \\( s = 9 - 2t \\) and \\( t = 3s + 1 \\), we need to solve the system of equations:\n\n\\[ s = 9 - 2t \\]\n\\[ t = 3s + 1 \\]\n\nFirst, substitute \\( t \\) from the second equation into the first equation:\n\n\\[ s = 9 - 2(3s + 1) \\]\n\\[ s = 9 - 6s - 2 \\]\n\\[ 7s = 7 \\]\n\\[ s = 1 \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( s = 1 \\) back into the second equation to find \\( t \\):\n\n\\[ t = 3(1) + 1 \\]\n\\[ t = 3 + 1 \\]\n\\[ t = 4 \\]\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is \\( (1, 4) \\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( (1, 4) \\). I hope it is correct."
