"We need to find the points of intersection between the curves $y=x^2$ and the line $x+y=1$.\n\nSubstitute $y=x^2$ into the equation of the line:\n$$x + x^2 = 1$$\n$$x^2 + x - 1 = 0$$\n\nSolve the quadratic equation:\n$$x = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 4}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}$$\n\nThe points of intersection are $\\left(\\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}, \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{-1 - \\sqrt{5}}{2}, \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nThe distance between these two points is the difference between their $x$-coordinates:\n$$\\text{Distance} = \\left|\\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} - \\frac{-1 - \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right| = \\sqrt{5}$$\n\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{5}}"
