"To find the positive value of the expression \\(\\sqrt{x^3 - 2^y}\\) when \\(x = 5\\) and \\(y = 2\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute the values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) into the expression.\n2. Simplify the expression inside the square root.\n3. Take the square root of the simplified expression.\n\nFirst, substitute \\(x = 5\\) and \\(y = 2\\) into the expression:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{5^3 - 2^2}\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate the powers:\n\\[\n5^3 = 125\n\\]\n\\[\n2^2 = 4\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these values back into the expression:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{125 - 4}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{121}\n\\]\n\nFinally, take the square root of 121:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{121} = 11\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]"
