"We can use the formula for compound interest to find the interest earned in two years. The formula is:\n\n\\[ A = P(1 + r)^n \\]\n\nwhere \\( A \\) is the amount after \\( n \\) years, \\( P \\) is the principal amount, \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate, and \\( n \\) is the number of years.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( P = 10000 \\)\n- \\( r = 0.045 \\)\n- \\( n = 2 \\)\n\nFirst, we calculate the amount after two years:\n\n\\[ A = 10000(1 + 0.045)^2 = 10000 \\times 1.0901 = 10901 \\]\n\nThe interest earned is the amount after the years minus the principal:\n\n\\[ \\text{Interest} = A - P = 10901 - 10000 = 901 \\]\n\nTherefore, the interest earned in two years is \\(\\boxed{901}\\) dollars."
