"To determine the greatest possible total number of intersection points of ten circles, we need to consider the maximum number of intersection points each pair of circles can have. Each pair of circles can intersect at most at two points.\n\nGiven that there are ten circles, we need to calculate the total number of pairs of circles. The number of ways to choose 2 circles out of 9 is given by the combination formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n\\]\n\nThus, there are 45 pairs of circles. Since each pair can intersect at most at two points, the total number of intersection points would be:\n\n\\[\n45 \\times 2 = 90\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the greatest possible total number of intersection points of these ten circles is \\(\\boxed{90}\\)."
